<section id="mthBlobClsOOSQLResult" revisionflag="changed"><title>&changed11;blob (Class method)</title>
<indexterm><primary>blob</primary></indexterm>
<indexterm><primary>ooSQLResult class</primary><secondary>blob</secondary></indexterm>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject>
<imagedata fileref="images/extensions/result_blob.svg" scale="55" />
</imageobject>
</mediaobject>

<para>
  Sets the result of a user defined SQL function, or of the final step of an aggregate, to a blob. It is used inside the
  Rexx callback methods registered with <link linkend="mthCreateFunction">createFunction</link>.
</para>
<variablelist>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Arguments:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    The arguments are:
  </para>
    <variablelist>
      <varlistentry><term>sqlCntxt [required]</term>
      <listitem>
      <para>
        The SQLite context, the second argument of the callback method.
      </para>
      </listitem></varlistentry>
      <varlistentry revisionflag="changed"><term>&changed11;blob [required]</term>
      <listitem>
      <para>
        The data of the blob: a String, a Buffer, or a MutableBuffer object, as for the bindBlob method of the
        ooSQLiteStmt class, or a Pointer object to the data. Before ooSQLite 1.1 only a Pointer was accepted, so Rexx
        code could not return a blob made from its own data, such as the String that the blob method of the <link
        linkend="clsOOSQLValue">ooSQLValue</link> class returns.
      </para>
      </listitem></varlistentry>
      <varlistentry revisionflag="changed"><term>&changed11;size [optional with a String, Buffer, or MutableBuffer, required with a Pointer]</term>
      <listitem>
      <para>
        The number of bytes of the blob. With a String, a Buffer, or a MutableBuffer, the blob is made of the first
        <emphasis role="italic">size</emphasis> bytes of the data, which must be from 0 to the length of the data; if
        it is omitted, all the data is used. With a Pointer it is the number of bytes to copy from that address.
      </para>
      </listitem></varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Return value:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    Returns 0. SQLite copies the data, so it can be discarded after the call.
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Details</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    The functionality of the <emphasis role="italic">blob</emphasis> method is similar to that of the SQLite
    <ulink url="http://www.sqlite.org/c3ref/result_blob.html"><citetitle>sqlite3_result_blob</citetitle></ulink> API.
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Example:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    This example defines a SQL function, <computeroutput>fromHex</computeroutput>, that turns a hexadecimal string into
    a blob:
  </para>
<programlisting language="rexx">
db = .ooSQLiteConnection~new(":memory:")
rc = db~createFunction("fromHex", .HexFunctions~new, 1, "fromHex")
rows = db~exec("SELECT typeof(fromHex('CAFE')) AS t, hex(fromHex('CAFE')) AS h;", .true)
say rows[1]["T"] rows[1]["H"]                    -- blob CAFE

::requires "ooSQLite.cls"

::class HexFunctions
::method fromHex
  use arg db, context, userData, value
  .ooSQLResult~blob(context, .ooSQLValue~text(value)~x2c)
  return 0
</programlisting>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</section>  <!-- End ooSQLResult::blob() -->




